      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%89rd"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s coat‑of‑arms shows three crowns, echoing the influence of the Ákos‑házy, Illésházy and Batthyány families.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%89rd"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 2008, Érd opened a dedicated Local History and Sports History Exhibition, housed in a 19th‑century house on Felső utca.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15,
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://foldrajzimuzeum.hu/erd-local-history-exhibition/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Turks seized Érd in 1543 after Székesfehérvár fell, building a motte castle and a mosque that still stands.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%89rd"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The mosque’s minaret, erected in the 17th century, is one of only three surviving Ottoman minarets in Hungary.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%89rd"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Érd’s name likely derives from the Hungarian word for forest, erdő, or from ér meaning stream.",
